\title{Finite Two-Colour Ramsey Theory: Exact Small Values, an Exact Infinite
Family, and the Erd\H{o}s--Szekeres Bound}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{June 21, 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We develop, from first principles, the elementary theory of finite two-colour
Ramsey numbers in a uniform graph-theoretic framework in which a two-colouring of
a complete graph is encoded by a single graph $G$ (its \emph{red} subgraph), the
\emph{blue} subgraph being the complement $G^{\cmpl}$. The central object is the
\emph{arrow relation} $n \to (s,t)$, asserting that every red/blue colouring of
any vertex set of size at least $n$ contains a red $s$-clique or a blue
$t$-clique. We establish four interlocking, fully proved results:
(i) the Erd\H{o}s--Szekeres recursion and the binomial upper bound
$R(s{+}1,t{+}1)\le\binom{s+t}{s}$;
(ii) the exact small value $R(3,3)=6$, with lower bound witnessed by the
self-complementary pentagon $C_5$;
(iii) the exact infinite family $R(2,t)=t$; and
(iv) the colour symmetry $n\to(s,t)\Rightarrow n\to(t,s)$.
The treatment is self-contained: the recursion is a single vertex-splitting
argument, the upper bound is Pascal's rule, and the lower bounds are explicit
extremal colourings. We close with algorithmic content, numerical
demonstrations, and the research frontier.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Ramsey theory makes precise the principle that \emph{complete disorder is
impossible}: any sufficiently large structure, however partitioned, contains a
large homogeneous substructure. Given two target sizes $s,t$, there is a least
integer $R(s,t)$ --- the \emph{Ramsey number} --- such that every red/blue
colouring of the edges of the complete graph $K_{R(s,t)}$ contains a red clique
on $s$ vertices or a blue clique on $t$ vertices, while some colouring of
$K_{R(s,t)-1}$ contains neither.

Exact values are notoriously scarce: the known diagonal values include only
$R(3,3)=6$ and $R(4,4)=18$, and $R(5,5)$ remains unknown. This scarcity is what
makes the few exact results, and the general bounds framing them, worth a fully
rigorous development. We present four contributions, carried out over an
arbitrary ambient vertex type for maximal reusability: the Erd\H{o}s--Szekeres
step and binomial bound (\S\ref{sec:es}); the exact value $R(3,3)=6$
(\S\ref{sec:33}); the exact family $R(2,t)=t$ (\S\ref{sec:2t}); and colour
symmetry (\S\ref{sec:symm}).

\section{The arrow framework}

\begin{definition}[Red/blue colouring]
A two-colouring of the complete graph on a vertex set $V$ is encoded by a simple
graph $G$ on $V$. The edges of $G$ are \textbf{red}; the edges of the complement
$G^{\cmpl}$ are \textbf{blue}. A \emph{red $s$-clique} is an $s$-element clique
of $G$; a \emph{blue $t$-clique} is a $t$-element clique of $G^{\cmpl}$. The
ambient type for the diagonal-shaped problem is
$\mathrm{ArrowsType}\,s\,t := \mathrm{SimpleGraph}\,(\Fin(s+t))$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Arrow relation]\label{def:arrows}
For $n,s,t\in\NN$,
\[
\Arrows\,n\,s\,t \ :\equiv\
\forall V,\ \forall G:\mathrm{SimpleGraph}\,V,\ \forall W:\mathrm{Finset}\,V,\
n\le |W| \implies
\]
\[
\bigl(\exists S\subseteq W,\ G.\IsNClique\,s\,S\bigr)\ \vee\
\bigl(\exists S\subseteq W,\ G^{\cmpl}.\IsNClique\,t\,S\bigr).
\]
We write $n\to(s,t)$ for $\Arrows\,n\,s\,t$, and set
$R(s,t) := \min\{\,n : n\to(s,t)\,\}$.
\end{definition}

Quantifying over an arbitrary vertex type $V$ together with a finite vertex set
$W$ of size at least $n$ bakes monotonicity into the definition and makes the
recursion state cleanly: the two recursive calls live on \emph{subsets} of the
same vertex set.

\begin{lemma}[Monotonicity]\label{lem:mono}
If $n\to(s,t)$ and $n\le n'$, then $n'\to(s,t)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If $|W|\ge n'\ge n$, apply the hypothesis to $W$.
\end{proof}

\section{Trivial base cases}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:onered}
$1\to(1,b)$ for every $b$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{lemma}\label{lem:oneblue}
$1\to(a,1)$ for every $a$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof of Lemmas \ref{lem:onered}--\ref{lem:oneblue}]
A nonempty $W$ contains a vertex $v$; the singleton $\{v\}$ is simultaneously a
red $1$-clique and a blue $1$-clique, since a one-element set is vacuously a
clique in any graph.
\end{proof}

\section{The Erd\H{o}s--Szekeres recursion and the binomial bound}\label{sec:es}

\begin{theorem}[Erd\H{o}s--Szekeres step]\label{thm:step}
If $m>0$, $n>0$, $m\to(s,t{+}1)$, and $n\to(s{+}1,t)$, then
\[ m+n \ \to\ (s{+}1,\ t{+}1). \]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Let $|W|\ge m+n\ge 1$ and choose $v\in W$. Partition $W\setminus\{v\}$ by the
colour of the edge to $v$:
\[
R = \{x\in W\setminus\{v\} : G.\Adj\,v\,x\},\quad
B = \{x\in W\setminus\{v\} : \neg\,G.\Adj\,v\,x\},
\]
so $|R|+|B| = |W|-1 \ge m+n-1$, whence $|R|\ge m$ or $|B|\ge n$.

If $|R|\ge m$: apply $m\to(s,t{+}1)$ to $R$. A blue $(t{+}1)$-clique finishes.
Otherwise a red $s$-clique $S\subseteq R$ appears; each vertex of $S$ is
red-adjacent to $v$ and $v\notin S$, so $S\cup\{v\}$ is a red $(s{+}1)$-clique.

If $|B|\ge n$: symmetric, applying $n\to(s{+}1,t)$ to $B$; a blue $t$-clique
extends by $v$ to a blue $(t{+}1)$-clique.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Erd\H{o}s--Szekeres binomial bound]\label{thm:bound}
For all $s,t\in\NN$,
\[ \binom{s+t}{s} \ \to\ (s{+}1,\ t{+}1), \qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad
R(s{+}1,t{+}1) \le \binom{s+t}{s}. \]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Double induction on $s,t$. Bases: $\binom{t}{0}=1$ with $1\to(1,t{+}1)$
(Lemma~\ref{lem:onered}), and $\binom{s}{s}=1$ with $1\to(s{+}1,1)$
(Lemma~\ref{lem:oneblue}). Step: from
$\binom{s+(t{+}1)}{s}\to(s{+}1,t{+}2)$ and
$\binom{(s{+}1)+t}{s{+}1}\to(s{+}2,t{+}1)$, Theorem~\ref{thm:step} gives the sum
arrowing to $(s{+}2,t{+}2)$, and by Pascal's rule
$\binom{s+t+1}{s}+\binom{s+t+1}{s+1}=\binom{s+t+2}{s+1}$ the threshold is exactly
the required binomial coefficient.
\end{proof}

\section{The exact value $R(3,3)=6$}\label{sec:33}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:33up}
$6\to(3,3)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The case $s=t=2$ of Theorem~\ref{thm:bound}: $\binom{4}{2}=6$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Pentagon]
Let $C_5$ be the graph on $\Fin 5$ with $a\sim b$ iff $a+1=b$ or $b+1=a$ (indices
mod $5$): the $5$-cycle.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:notri}
$C_5$ contains no red triangle, and $C_5^{\cmpl}$ contains no blue triangle.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Finite verification. The $5$-cycle has girth $5$, hence is triangle-free; its
complement is again a $5$-cycle (the pentagram) on five vertices, hence also
triangle-free. The pentagon is self-complementary.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:33low}
$5\not\to(3,3)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Instantiate Definition~\ref{def:arrows} with $G=C_5$ on $\Fin 5$. A red or blue
triangle would contradict Lemma~\ref{lem:notri}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$R(3,3)=6$]\label{thm:r33}
$6\to(3,3)$ and $5\not\to(3,3)$; hence $R(3,3)=6$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Combine Theorems~\ref{thm:33up} and~\ref{thm:33low}.
\end{proof}

\section{The exact infinite family $R(2,t)=t$}\label{sec:2t}

For $s=2$ a ``red clique'' is a single red edge, and the Ramsey number collapses
to a closed form, proved directly without the Erd\H{o}s--Szekeres machinery.

\begin{theorem}[Upper bound]\label{thm:2tup}
$t\to(2,t)$ for every $t$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Let $|W|\ge t$. If $G$ has a red edge $\{u,v\}$ inside $W$, that is a red
$2$-clique. Otherwise $W$ is independent in $G$, hence a clique in $G^{\cmpl}$,
and any $t$-element subset is a blue $t$-clique.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Clean lower bound]\label{thm:2tlow}
$t\not\to(2,t{+}1)$ for every $t$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Take $G=\bot$ (all edges blue) on $\Fin t$: no red edge, and only $t<t+1$
vertices for a blue $(t{+}1)$-clique.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:2tpred}
For $t\ge 1$, $t-1\not\to(2,t)$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Write $t=t'+1$ and apply Theorem~\ref{thm:2tlow} to $t'$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$R(2,t)=t$]\label{thm:r2t}
For $t\ge 1$, $t\to(2,t)$ and $(t-1)\not\to(2,t)$; hence $R(2,t)=t$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Combine Theorem~\ref{thm:2tup} and Corollary~\ref{cor:2tpred}.
\end{proof}

\section{Colour symmetry}\label{sec:symm}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:symm}
If $n\to(s,t)$ then $n\to(t,s)$; consequently $R(s,t)=R(t,s)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply the hypothesis to the complemented colouring $G^{\cmpl}$. It yields a red
$s$-clique of $G^{\cmpl}$ or a blue $t$-clique of $(G^{\cmpl})^{\cmpl}=G$; by
involutivity of complementation these are a blue $s$-clique of $G$ or a red
$t$-clique of $G$, which is exactly $n\to(t,s)$.
\end{proof}

\section{Algorithmic content}

\textbf{Vertex-splitting clique search.} Theorem~\ref{thm:step} is an algorithm:
pick $v$, split into red/blue neighbourhoods, recurse on the larger side, prepend
$v$. Unwinding Theorem~\ref{thm:bound} constructs a monochromatic clique of the
guaranteed size on $\binom{s+t}{s}$ vertices.

\textbf{Exhaustive small-case verification.} For fixed small $n$ the arrow
predicate is decidable: enumerate the $2^{\binom{n}{2}}$ colourings and search the
candidate cliques. This discharges the pentagon lemmas ($2^{10}=1024$ colourings)
and brute-force confirms $R(3,3)=6$ ($2^{15}=32768$ colourings).

\textbf{Binomial-bound table.} Pascal's rule computes $\binom{s+t}{s}$ in $O(st)$
operations, certifying upper bounds for all $R(s{+}1,t{+}1)$ at once.

\section{Numerical illustrations}

$R(3,3)=6$: all $2^{15}$ colourings of $K_6$ contain a monochromatic triangle,
while the pentagon colouring of $K_5$ does not. $R(2,t)=t$: the binomial bound
gives $\binom{t}{1}=t$, matching the exact value. Binomial bounds:
$R(3,4)\le\binom{5}{2}=10$ and $R(4,4)\le\binom{6}{3}=20$.

\section{Discussion and future directions}

The exact results here point directly at the frontier.
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Matching $R(4,4)=18$} via the Paley graph on $\GF(17)$
($i\sim j$ iff $i-j$ is a nonzero quadratic residue mod $17$), a
self-complementary strongly regular graph $\mathrm{srg}(17,8,3,4)$ with no $K_4$
in either colour; the algebraic parameters replace infeasible brute force.
\item \textbf{Off-diagonal probabilistic bounds}, e.g.
$R(3,t)=\Omega(t^2/\log t)$ via weighted/deletion union bounds.
\item \textbf{The probabilistic method}: if $\binom{n}{s}2^{1-\binom{s}{2}}<1$
then $n\not\to(s,s)$.
\item \textbf{Hales--Jewett}: combinatorial lines in high-dimensional grids.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

In a uniform arrow framework over arbitrary vertex types we gave self-contained
proofs of the Erd\H{o}s--Szekeres recursion and binomial bound, the exact value
$R(3,3)=6$ (pentagon lower bound), the exact family $R(2,t)=t$, and the colour
symmetry $R(s,t)=R(t,s)$. The content is elementary --- one vertex-splitting
recursion, Pascal's rule, and explicit extremal colourings --- yet furnishes a
complete, exact, reusable foundation.

